so just talk us through what happened  you know you're you're in Qatar as well  as first match you're in your hotel room  or wherever you're staying getting ready  for the match and you of course put on  your bucket hat  yeah well Buddha good morning first of  all Sophie yes I mean look I think the  first thing to say is it wasn't just me  that was in that predicament I mean I've  had a louder voice because of my role  here I'm an ambassador for Wales for the  Football Association of Wales and the  Welsh government so I guess I've had a  bigger platform  but there were others in the same  position there were several friends and  colleagues of ours who'd been uh who'd  had their hats removed and confiscated  or indeed in a couple of cases thrown in  the bin which is pretty insulting and of  course the the reality is we we were  told by FIFA before the tournament that  rainbow attire and rainbow symbols would  be permitted inside the stadiums quite  rightly so too so to be perfectly honest  I hadn't really thought that there would  be any kind of issue with entering the  stadium you know we would we were doing  this in a respectful way  um but we were making clear the values  that we stand for as a nation and for us  in Wales you know we're the smallest  country represented in this world cup  that's uh that's actually qualified and  it was really important for us to use  our role in the World Cup as a platform  to show what we're all about as a nation  and that's about human rights for  everyone and not excluding the LGBT  community from that so that's partly why  it was so disappointing rather than the  personal intimidation  what exactly did they say to you I'm  just intrigued you know when they  stopped you and you can see the video  there that happening what was the reason  that they gave  Well we'd had a word passed back to us  in the queue that some of our colleagues  from the faw and other fans had had  issues with their rainbow hat so a  couple of us were standing together  sensed that something was likely to  happen but obviously we weren't prepared  to take our hats off you know as I say  it's a important emblem if you like for  our commitment to Human Rights and and  equality for all but when I went through  the security scanners  um and I came through the other end one  of the female security guards pointed to  my hat and said take off and I said why  you know I have to emphasize we've been  really polite in all of this there was  no point at which we tried to seek any  confrontation I asked why and she simply  replied regulation so I asked which  regulation because obviously you know I  work with the faw and I I'm involved  with UEFA so I do know the regulations  um and she she couldn't really answer so  another lady came forward and said the  same take hat off or you can't enter the  stadium and again I asked it why and  they simply came up with word regulation  so I mean I stood my ground I just said  I don't know what you're referring to  but by then a male I think he he was  some kind of security manager uh came  around and and pointed again to the hat  and said if you don't take that off you  don't come in inside the stadium  you say you know you obviously did stand  your ground and you are someone who you  know you're a former Captain you are an  ambassador you're someone in a position  of some Authority but it must have felt  quite intimidating  uh well it wasn't the best experience  I've had of going into a a stadium and  you know I've been as well as being  involved in football I'm a fan first and  foremost you know I've I've been to  Wales game since I was a toddler  um and it's you know it's a pretty  unsavory start to what was the biggest  game in our country's history you know  we haven't been in a World Cup as you  know for 64 years so I was looking  forward to going in with my friends and  my colleagues from the Football  Association was to support uh the the  national team so it wasn't ideal but  there's so much else that I think we  have to stand up for in a in a country  like Qatar you know we know all of us  who are here are horribly compromised by  the political regime here we know that  you know they've been such Dreadful  issues around migrant workers we know  that women don't have basic rights as  they do in the west we know as well that  LGBT people are criminalized and so I  think you know for those of us who live  in countries like Wales where obviously  those rights are protected and promoted  you know it is incumbent on us as well  to standard ground and to show that you  know LGBT rights are human rights and  the other thing I'd say actually is that  you know people have said to me  um I think mostly we've had great  support for our Stan but if anyone was  trying to be critical they've said you  know are you not  disrespecting local cultures and Norms  here but I think there's a really  important point there which is this  isn't a Qatari World Cup this is the  FIFA World Cup in Qatar and FIFA is the  organizing uh body for this and they  assured everyone that that everyone  would be welcome that it was a World Cup  for the whole world whoever they are and  whatever their identity and of course  the advice and guidance around rainbow  symbols uh reflected that so I think you  know given all of that it was really  important that we stood up to some  pretty heavy-handed security within the  stadium  it's interesting you talk about people  making that argument and you know on  this program you know I've asked  government ministers about it and  they've said that it's important for  people to respect the laws in their  words of the host country do you agree  with that assessment or do you think  that the UK government should be a bit  stronger I guess  well it's a very delicate balance isn't  it you know I think there's an issue  over awarding uh major events to  countries that can't guarantee basic  human rights and that's one for FIFA  obviously but I mean we have to live our  values whilst we're here you know and  I've had nothing but support from our  government in Wales and indeed from from  the Welsh public and I think there's  lots of people at home who are trade  unionists who are feminists who are LGBT  uh people or activists or Allies and  they felt very uncomfortable about  coming to Qatar so those of us who are  here here in a work capacity have a duty  I think to speak up for them and to show  that you know that there is a an  alternative  equality if you like in our countries  which I think is really important you  know because otherwise how do we ever  have conversations about change and you  know I'm I don't want to be  disrespectful to anyone in Qatar but I  think the very fact that we've been able  to highlight this issue you know people  Qatar people and other nations have  talked to us as Wales fans wearing our  rainbow hats they've talked to us about  what we're about and what those symbols  represent and in a sense that's what a  major International Sports tournament  has to be about as well because there's  no there's no doubt at all in my mind  you know that it's nonsense to suggest  that politics and Sport are separate  they're not and that's the reason why  the world cup is here in the first place  of course so equally you can't then  remove politics once we come to a state  like Qatar and expect us not to be true  to the values that are important to us  in Wales and and of course in England  and in other countries which have much  more Progressive attitudes to this and  fair play to you as well for you know  doing this interview you're there in  Qatar and you are you know sort of  taking on if you like some of those uh  prejudices by giving us your view as  well and players have been registering  their protests in different ways we've  had some teams for example covering  their mouths to show that they couldn't  stay at what they wanted to but they've  stopped short of you know wearing the  rainbow armband and taking the yellow  card as punishment  do you think there should have been a  bit more I guess coordinated action we  had Greg Dyke on the program earlier he  said that look if people have been a bit  more organized all of the European teams  for example coming together saying that  they would have worn the rainbow armband  then actually  um they'd have just had to swallow it  well I think there's a few things to say  there you know I think it was  fundamentally wrong of FIFA to  intervene in the players environment on  Match day I mean that's unforgivable you  know the players are here to play  football and whatever the issue they  shouldn't be conversations with players  about what they wear on the field of  play on the day of a match or hours  before kickoff which of course is what  FIFA did with England and and indeed  with us in Wales  um I think we'll have to reflect on this  when we get back you know because I know  that Gareth Bale the the Wales Captain  was extremely keen and enthusiastic to  wear the one love arm band you know he  he felt it was an important reflection  of the the stuff that I've been talking  about really what what our nation is all  about but I think you know you have to  recognize as well that FIFA were  threatening Beyond red cards you know  they kept it an open-ended threat from  what I gather that it could be beyond  the yellow card so who knows how that  would have played out and I think that's  fundamentally wrong you know we've put  in the players in a really invidious  position we learned lessons from it  because I think in unity their strength  and the Seven National associations  those who've got Progressive equality  approaches should have probably thought  about a plan b or what might happen  given we hadn't hadn't had a formal  response from FIFA about the one love  Arm bans but you know we will reflect on  that certainly in Wales where these  these matters uh these matters are very  very important to us but but I have to  say you know as a former player myself  on Match day you know the attention of  players should be on the biggest game in  their careers and for those Welsh  players you know they were the first  players to represent Wales in a World  Cup for six decades and I know they  wanted to do their best on equality  issues but it was very very wrong of  FIFA to put them in that position  talking about the biggest game of  people's careers at Wells of course  playing England I'm not going to ask if  you're going to watch the game because I  certainly know the answer to that one  um but do you think whilst I've got a  chance of beating England  well of course we've got a chance  um but we're going to have to play a lot  better than we've played in the first  two games  um I think the fans will they won't be  optimistic but they'll still believe  I've spoken to a lot of my friends who  are here who still think that we can get  a result against England we've got to  Hope of course at the Iran USA game is a  draw then but you know we we haven't  beaten England for a long time so we do  we're do a win but you know make no  mistaking that England are a great team  they've got strength in depth they've  got such quality on the bench and at the  moment we're not firing on on Old  cylinders but but things change very  quickly in football you know if we go  out and put in a performance yes we can  beat them but you know I think from most  Welsh fans perspectives it's the  performance now we've been disappointed  on the pitch for whatever reason and I  think if we can go out and play well and  with real passion and energy against  England I think the result will come but  even if it doesn't come uh the fact the  fans will be proud of the the team and  go home you know happy that we've shown  what we're about out on the world stage  um great thank you so much for being on  the program today you know you've been  such a sort of strong voice for equality  and for you know the values that you  clearly hold very dear as well in Qatar  so thank you

